WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.152
A common misunderstanding is to think of Spark as PostgreSQL but bigger.

00:00:04.152 --> 00:00:05.702
Spark is different.

00:00:05.702 --> 00:00:12.517
PostgreSQL is a database system with transactions, constraints, indexes, and stored tables.

00:00:12.517 --> 00:00:15.282
Spark is a distributed compute engine.

00:00:15.282 --> 00:00:21.007
It can query tables and files, but its strength is large-scale analytical processing.

00:00:21.007 --> 00:00:27.402
In many real architectures, a database and Spark work together rather than replacing each other.

00:00:27.402 --> 00:00:32.397
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:32.397 --> 00:00:36.467
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:36.467 --> 00:00:40.457
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:40.457 --> 00:00:47.072
In a real setting such as a healthcare analytics platform processing large longitudinal event tables,

00:00:47.072 --> 00:00:52.952
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:52.952 --> 00:00:55.007
before discussing syntax.

00:00:55.007 --> 00:01:01.090
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
